@database "basic_agent"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: basic_agent.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Basic agent"
@{h1}Basic agent

This plugin is the plain agent: it takes a message, runs the LLM loop with
the tools the agent is allowed to use, and answers. It has no tools of its
own for the chat and no prompt of its own -- what an agent of this type can
do comes entirely from its configuration: its LLM profiles, its system
prompt, its tool list and its hook settings. Many agents in the picker are
of this type or inherit from one that is -- @{tt}chat_agent@{utt}, the
default, among them. When another agent is allowed to, it can call such an
agent as a tool and hand it a task. The plugin has no panel and no hooks.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Chatting with it  " link using}

For agents and developers
  @{"  Setting up an agent  " link setup}
  @{"  execute_task  " link execute_task}
  @{"  list_available_tools  " link list_available_tools}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
@{body}
@endnode

@node using "Chatting with it"
@{h1}Chatting with it

An agent of this type is an ordinary chat partner. Pick it with the
@{b}Agent@{ub} button under the message box, or type
@{tt}/agent NAME@{utt}; see @{" Choosing an agent " link scarabhive/agents}.
What it knows and can do is set by whoever configured it:

@{bullet}@{b}Its tools@{ub}: @{tt}/tools@{utt} lists what it really has.
@{bullet}@{b}Its model@{ub}: it runs its own chain of LLM profiles unless
the @{b}Model profile@{ub} button picks another; see
@{" LLM models and profiles " link scarabhive/llm}.
@{bullet}@{b}Its history@{ub}: every conversation is a session, see
@{" Sessions " link scarabhive/sessions}.

Nothing in the chat is specific to this plugin; everything the web UI shows
is described in @{" The web UI " link scarabhive/webui}.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting up an agent"
@{h1}Setting up an agent

An agent of this type is a server entry with @{tt}type: basic_agent@{utt}
-- or with the name of another such entry, whose configuration it
inherits. The plugin needs no entry of its own. The shipped
@{tt}chat_agent@{utt} (@{tt}config/agents/agents.yaml@{utt}):

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    chat_agent:
      type: basic_agent
      enabled: true
      description: "Simple chat agent"

      agent_config:
        llm_profile: [chat, or-gemini-flash-lite]
        llm_profile_advanced: []
        max_steps: 30

        hooks:
          enabled: true
          overrides:
            markdown_formatter.format_markdown_output:
              enabled: true

      metadata:
        visibility: "both"
@{body}

Every key of @{tt}agent_config@{utt} -- the prompt, @{tt}max_steps@{utt},
the tool allowlist, skills, timeouts -- belongs to the core agent, not to
this plugin; they are described in
@{" Writing an agent " link scarabhive/agentdev}. Entries without their own
values take them from @{tt}plugins.default_config@{utt} in
@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} (two profiles @{tt}[normal, think]@{utt},
20 steps, @{tt}config/prompts/system_prompt.md@{utt}, the
markdown_formatter hooks switched off).

@{h2}What this plugin reads itself

@{table}
Key | Where | Meaning
agent_config.llm_profile | the entry | the default chain; its profiles are what the @{tt}llm_profile@{utt} argument may pick
agent_config.llm_profile_advanced | the entry | the chain @{tt}use_advanced_model@{utt} runs on; empty: the argument does nothing
agent_config.llm_params | the entry | the agent's model settings; a profile picked by argument gets them too
metadata.visibility | the entry | @{tt}tool@{utt} or @{tt}both@{utt}: other agents may call it as a tool
@{body}

@{tt}self_tool_descriptions: {<instance>_execute_task: "..."}@{utt} in the
entry (beside @{tt}type@{utt}) replaces a tool's description for that
agent, so a caller's model learns what this agent is for.

@{h2}Hooks

The plugin brings none. The agent's run fires the hooks of every enabled
hook plugin; which of them apply to this agent, and with what settings, is
its @{tt}agent_config.hooks@{utt} block, see
@{" Hooks " link scarabhive/hooks}.

@{h2}Called by another agent

For another agent to use this one as a tool, both sides agree: this
agent's @{tt}metadata.visibility@{utt} is @{tt}tool@{utt} or
@{tt}both@{utt}, and the caller allows it in its own list:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+chat_agent/*"
@{body}

The caller then has @{tt}chat_agent_execute_task@{utt} and
@{tt}chat_agent_list_available_tools@{utt}. For a sub-agent the caller
starts and talks to over several turns, with limits and cancelling, the
sub-agent manager is the better way: see
@{" Sub-agents and the SAM " link scarabhive/subagents}.

@{h2}Dependencies and data

None beyond the core. The server is built when the system starts. The
agent's conversations are sessions like any other; the plugin stores
nothing of its own.
@endnode

@node execute_task "execute_task"
@{h1}execute_task

@{tt}<instance>_execute_task@{utt} hands the agent one task and waits for
its answer. The agent runs its whole loop -- LLM calls, its own tools --
and the call returns when it is done.

@{h2}Parameters

The framework does not check the arguments against the schema; what
follows is what the code does with them.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
task | required; the task as text. Missing or empty: an error
llm_profile | offered only when the agent has more than one profile in its two chains together, and then only those. Runs on that profile with the agent's own llm_params; the agent's default chain is the fallback. Wins over use_advanced_model
use_advanced_model | true runs on the first profile of @{tt}llm_profile_advanced@{utt}, with the advanced chain and then the default chain as fallback. No effect when that chain is empty, starts with the default profile, or llm_profile is given; default false
@{body}

Without either argument the agent runs its default chain -- or, when its
@{tt}agent_config.inherit_parent_llm@{utt} is true and the calling run was
switched to another profile, on the caller's profile.

@{h2}Its session

Called from a chat, the agent does not work in the caller's session but in
one of its own below it, one per caller session and agent: a second call
from the same conversation sees the first call's task and answer. Its user
is the caller's. Called without a session (a plugin command, a script),
every call starts a new one.

@{h2}The answer

@{table}
Field | Meaning
status | success, error or cancelled
result | the agent's final answer; "Task completed successfully" when it answered with nothing
tool_calls | every tool call of the run: tool (the plugin instance), action (the tool name), params (the arguments without the framework's @{tt}_@{utt} keys)
steps | 1 plus the number of tool calls
request_id | the id the run went under
error | on error and cancelled: why
error_type | on some errors: which refusal (see below)
@{body}

@{h2}Errors

An error comes back as @{tt}{"status": "error", "error": ...}@{utt}; the
tool does not raise.

@{table}
error | When
Missing required parameter 'task' | no task, or an empty one
LLM profile '<p>' not available for this agent. Available profiles: [...] | llm_profile names a profile outside the agent's chains
LLM profile '<p>' not found in system configuration. Available system profiles: [...] | the profile is in the agent's chain but not under llm_system.profiles
Failed to initialize LLM profile '<p>': <reason> | the client could not be built (a missing key, an unknown provider)
<agent> is running above this call already (in session <s>): an agent does not call itself, ... | error_type recursive_call: the agent would call itself, directly or through other agents
Session <s> belongs to another user | error_type foreign_session
Session <s> is currently locked by another request (<r>). Please wait for that request to complete. | error_type session_locked: a second call from the same conversation while the first still runs
the run's own error | the loop failed: max steps reached without an answer, a timeout, empty answers, ... -- with its error_type where the run gives one
@{body}

When the run is stopped, the answer is @{tt}{"status": "cancelled",
"cancelled": true, "error": "Task cancelled at step <n>"}@{utt} with the
tool calls made until then.

@{h2}An example

@{code json}
{"task": "Which weekday is it in Tokyo right now?"}
@{body}

@{code json}
{"status": "success", "result": "It is Thursday in Tokyo.",
 "tool_calls": [{"tool": "datetime", "action": "datetime_operations",
                 "params": {"operation": "current", "timezone": "Asia/Tokyo"}}],
 "steps": 2, "request_id": "a1b2c3_4"}
@{body}
@endnode

@node list_available_tools "list_available_tools"
@{h1}list_available_tools

@{tt}<instance>_list_available_tools@{utt} takes no arguments and answers
with a list of @{tt}{"name": ..., "description": ...}@{utt}, one per tool
the agent may call -- exactly the list its own LLM gets, after the
allowlist, the blocklist and visibility. Deferred tools
(@{tt}tools.deferred@{utt}) are in it although a run sends their schema
only once the model loads them. A failure while listing is an error of the
call, not an empty list.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

@{h2}The tools

The two tools come from the plugin's @{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}, rendered per
agent: the names carry the instance's name, the @{tt}llm_profile@{utt}
parameter appears only with more than one profile and lists them, and
@{tt}use_advanced_model@{utt} tells the model it has no effect when the
agent has no advanced profile other than its default. The rendered tools
are kept for the life of the server; a change to the profiles needs a
restart.

@{h2}The chat status line

While @{tt}execute_task@{utt} runs: "Using LLM profile: <profile>" when
one was picked, "Starting basic agent task: <task, 100 characters>...",
"Step <n> - Using <tool> (<action>)" per tool call, "Processing results
from <tool>...", "Finalizing results...". At the end: "<n> step(s), <m>
tool call(s), <k> chars -- <task, 60 characters>"; on failure "Task
failed: <error>", "Task execution failed: <error>" or "Task cancelled at
step <n>". A refusal before the run -- a recursive call, another user's
session, a session that cannot be filed -- and a bad @{tt}llm_profile@{utt}
show the bare error; a locked session and the role gate come from the run
and show "Task failed: <error>". @{tt}list_available_tools@{utt} ends with "Listed available
tools (<n> tools)".

@{h2}Tokens and cache

The caller gets one tool result, appended to its conversation; nothing
earlier is rewritten, so its prompt cache stays intact. The result holds
the final answer and every tool call's arguments -- a run with many large
arguments (files written, long queries) makes a long result. The called
agent's own LLM calls run in its own session and are counted there.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}@{tt}steps@{utt} does not count LLM calls; it is the tool calls
plus one.
@endnode
